---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="BMI calculator: body mass index and the healthy weight range for your height"
  description="Work out your body mass index from your height and weight, see which band it falls in, and the weight range the WHO counts as healthy for your height."
>
  <h1>BMI calculator</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Body mass index is a rough sorting tool: your weight divided by your height squared. It
    puts most adults into one of four bands. It is a starting point for a conversation with a
    doctor, not a diagnosis, and this page treats it that way.
  </p>
    <figure class="fig fig--health"><img src="/img/bmi.webp" alt="A person lifting a barbell in a gym" width="1200" height="514" decoding="async"></figure>

  <div class="sheet">
    <div class="field-grid">
      <div>
        <label for="unit">Units</label>
        <select id="unit">
          <option value="metric">Kilograms and centimetres</option>
          <option value="imperial">Pounds, feet and inches</option>
        </select>
      </div>
      <div id="metric-fields">
        <label for="kg">Weight (kg)</label>
        <input type="number" id="kg" inputmode="decimal" min="20" max="500" step="0.1" placeholder="70" />
        <label for="cm" style="margin-top:0.75rem;">Height (cm)</label>
        <input type="number" id="cm" inputmode="decimal" min="80" max="250" step="0.5" placeholder="175" />
      </div>
      <div id="imperial-fields" hidden>
        <label for="lb">Weight (lb)</label>
        <input type="number" id="lb" inputmode="decimal" min="45" max="1100" step="0.5" placeholder="154" />
        <label for="ft" style="margin-top:0.75rem;">Height (feet)</label>
        <input type="number" id="ft" inputmode="numeric" min="2" max="8" step="1" placeholder="5" />
        <label for="inch" style="margin-top:0.75rem;">Height (inches)</label>
        <input type="number" id="inch" inputmode="decimal" min="0" max="11.9" step="0.5" placeholder="9" />
      </div>
    </div>
    <button class="btn btn--primary" id="calc" type="button">Work out my BMI</button>
    <div id="out" aria-live="polite" style="margin-top:1.5rem;">
      <p class="result-note">Enter your height and weight and the answer appears here.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="result-note">
      This is general information, not medical advice. BMI does not measure body fat, muscle,
      or where fat sits, and it misleads for athletes, older people, children and during
      pregnancy. If the result worries you, take it to a doctor, not to a diet you found online.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The formula and the bands</h2>
  <p>
    BMI = weight in kilograms divided by height in metres, squared. With pounds and inches
    the same idea carries a conversion factor: BMI = 703 × pounds ÷ inches². The bands below
    are the World Health Organization ranges for adults, used by the NHS and the CDC.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Band</th><th scope="col">What it usually means</th><th scope="col" class="num">BMI</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Underweight</td><td>Below the range linked to the lowest health risk</td><td class="num">under 18.5</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Healthy weight</td><td>The reference range for most adults</td><td class="num">18.5 to 24.9</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Overweight</td><td>Risk of weight-related illness starts to climb</td><td class="num">25.0 to 29.9</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Obese</td><td>Risk is materially higher, worth a doctor conversation</td><td class="num">30.0 and over</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>One example, worked by hand</h2>
  <p>
    A person who weighs 70 kg and is 175 cm tall. Height in metres is 1.75, and 1.75 squared
    is 3.0625. Divide 70 by 3.0625 and you get 22.86, which rounds to <strong>22.9</strong>,
    inside the healthy band. For that height, the healthy band starts at 18.5 × 3.0625 =
    56.7 kg and ends at 24.9 × 3.0625 = 76.3 kg. In imperial units the same person, 154 lb and
    5 feet 9 inches (69 inches), gives 703 × 154 ÷ 4,761 = 22.7. The small difference is
    rounding in the conversion, nothing more.
  </p>

  <h2>Questions people ask</h2>
  <dl class="faq">
    <dt>Why does BMI get criticised so much?</dt>
    <dd>
      Because it cannot tell muscle from fat, and it says nothing about waist size, fitness or
      blood pressure. A rugby player and a sedentary office worker can share a BMI of 27 with
      very different health. Doctors still use it because it is free, instant, and roughly
      right across whole populations.
    </dd>
    <dt>Is the healthy range the same for everyone?</dt>
    <dd>
      No. The WHO bands are drawn for adults of European background. Several health bodies use
      lower action points, 23 and 27.5, for people of South Asian, Chinese and some other
      backgrounds, because risk rises earlier. Children use age and sex percentiles instead,
      and this calculator is not built for them.
    </dd>
    <dt>How much should I weigh for my height?</dt>
    <dd>
      The calculator prints the healthy weight range for your height under your result. Treat
      the middle of that range as a rough landmark, not a target you owe anyone.
    </dd>
  </dl>

  <p class="meta">Sources: World Health Organization BMI classification; CDC adult BMI categories. Page last checked 13 November 2026.</p>

  <script>
    const unit = document.getElementById('unit');
    const mf = document.getElementById('metric-fields');
    const imf = document.getElementById('imperial-fields');
    const out = document.getElementById('out');

    unit.addEventListener('change', () => {
      const imp = unit.value === 'imperial';
      mf.hidden = imp;
      imf.hidden = !imp;
    });

    function band(bmi) {
      if (bmi < 18.5) return 'underweight';
      if (bmi < 25) return 'in the healthy weight band';
      if (bmi < 30) return 'in the overweight band';
      return 'in the obese band';
    }

    function compute() {
      let kg, m;
      if (unit.value === 'metric') {
        kg = parseFloat(document.getElementById('kg').value);
        const cm = parseFloat(document.getElementById('cm').value);
        m = cm / 100;
      } else {
        const lb = parseFloat(document.getElementById('lb').value);
        const ft = parseFloat(document.getElementById('ft').value) || 0;
        const inch = parseFloat(document.getElementById('inch').value) || 0;
        kg = lb * 0.45359237;
        m = (ft * 12 + inch) * 0.0254;
      }
      if (!kg || !m || kg <= 0 || m <= 0) {
        out.innerHTML = '<p class="result-note">Enter both your height and your weight and the answer appears here.</p>';
        return;
      }
      const bmi = kg / (m * m);
      const lo = 18.5 * m * m;
      const hi = 24.9 * m * m;
      const fmt = (n, d) => n.toLocaleString('en-GB', { minimumFractionDigits: d, maximumFractionDigits: d });
      let rangeText;
      if (unit.value === 'metric') {
        rangeText = 'For your height, the healthy weight range is about ' + fmt(lo, 1) + ' kg to ' + fmt(hi, 1) + ' kg.';
      } else {
        rangeText = 'For your height, the healthy weight range is about ' + fmt(lo / 0.45359237, 0) + ' lb to ' + fmt(hi / 0.45359237, 0) + ' lb.';
      }
      out.innerHTML =
        '<p class="result-figure">BMI ' + fmt(bmi, 1) + '</p>' +
        '<p>That puts you ' + band(bmi) + ' on the WHO scale for adults. ' + rangeText + '</p>' +
        '<p class="result-note">A single number is a rough sort, not a verdict on your health. Muscle, waist size, age and background all change what it means.</p>';
    }

    document.getElementById('calc').addEventListener('click', compute);
  </script>
</Base>
